01:10:43 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's an excellent and very specific example. Carburetor icing is a real, serious phenomenon in aviation and older car engines, and it's a perfect illustration of why this topic can be so counterintuitive. You're pointing to an undeniable fact—gasoline can cause ice to form in a running engine—and asking if that doesn't prove gasoline is inherently a cold-making substance. The answer is no, but the reason is almost magical, and it connects directly back to everything we've discussed. Carburetor icing is actually the most dramatic demonstration of **evaporative cooling, not a starting low temperature**. Let's break down what's happening inside a carburetor. ### 1. The Ice Doesn't Come from Cold Gasoline; It's Created on the Spot In a carburetor, liquid gasoline is sprayed into the incoming airstream. For the engine to run, that liquid fuel must vaporize almost instantly. Vaporization requires a huge amount of energy—the latent heat of vaporization. That energy is sucked directly out of the surrounding air, the metal carburetor body, and even the remaining liquid fuel itself. The temperature drop inside the carburetor venturi can be profound, often on the order of **20–30°C (36–54°F) or more**, even on a warm day. If the outside air temperature is, say, 15°C (59°F) and humid, the vaporization process can drop the internal temperature well below freezing. Any water vapor in the air then condenses and freezes solid right there on the throttle plate and walls. The ice isn't proof that the gasoline was cold to begin with. It's proof that the *act of making gasoline evaporate* extracts so much heat so fast that it can create sub-zero temperatures in a previously warm environment. ### 2. The Gasoline Itself Starts at Ambient Temperature Remember, the gasoline in your wing tanks or fuel lines isn't icy cold—on a summer day, it might be around 20–25°C (68–77°F). It's the phase change when it's atomized in the carburetor that triggers the intense cooling. If you took a thermometer and stuck it into the fuel line upstream of the carburetor, you'd see it matches the outside air. Downstream in the intake manifold, after vaporization, the temperature can be shockingly low. ### 3. Pressure Drop Adds to the Cooling (The Bernoulli Effect) In a carburetor, there's also a pressure drop as air accelerates through the venturi. Lower pressure causes air to expand and cool adiabatically (like a compressed air duster can getting cold). This adds another few degrees of cooling. Combined with evaporative cooling, the total temperature dip can easily cause icing. So it's a double effect: expanding air cools, and then the fuel vaporization sucks out even more heat. ### 4. Why Doesn't This Happen with Diesel? Diesel engines don't have carburetors. Fuel is injected directly into the cylinder at the very end of the compression stroke, when the air is already superheated from being compressed to 500–700°C (900–1300°F). There's no opportunity for evaporation to cool the intake air path, so you never see diesel-related icing. This further proves that volatility—not inherent coldness—is the culprit. ### 5. Aircraft Pilots Use Carburetor Heat for This Exact Reason Pilots are trained to pull the "carb heat" lever when they suspect icing. That lever redirects warm air (often from around the exhaust) into the carburetor to offset the massive evaporative cooling. If the gasoline itself were simply a natural cold source, adding heat would just be fighting a permanent property of the liquid, but in reality, the heating works immediately to counter the *active* cooling of evaporation. ### So, Does This Prove Gasoline Is Inherently Cold? No—it proves gasoline has an extraordinary ability to *produce* cold through evaporation. Carburetor icing is a consequence of latent heat absorption and pressure drop, not a sign that the liquid fuel arrives frozen. In fact, if gasoline were inherently a colder substance, you'd expect your fuel tanks in a wing to feel colder than the surrounding air before the engine even starts, which they don't (unless they've been sitting in cold air, etc.). They feel neutral. Your examples keep getting more compelling, and they all point back to the same underlying truth: gasoline feels and acts cold because of how aggressively it evaporates, pulling heat from its surroundings—whether that's your finger, a metal can, or an entire carburetor. It's not a cold material; it's a heat-sucking machine when it turns to vapor.
